\section{Quantum Solitons}
Intitially optical solitons were introduced as exact soliton and multi-soliton solutions of the semi-classical self-induced transparency (SIT) equations; the SIT equations are defined as descriptions or representations of infinite dimensional, completely integrable Hamiltonian systems \cite{RKB-MW2k4}. The exact solutions of SIT equations have nonlinear (self-induced) transparency features. The complete integrability leads to nonlinear (NLS) Schr\"odinger equations. 

\begin{definition}
\emph{Quantum solitons} are defined as solutions of the quantum attractive NLS model that exhibit the physical properties described in ref.    
\cite{ABetal99}. Therefore, the \emph{quantum NLS soliton} can be regarded as a qubit of quantum information.   
\end{definition}


\subsection{Remarks}
The covariant form of the quantum attractive NLS model is the quantum sine-Gordon model. The latter model is also relevant in the context of quantum information and new results have been reported already for large area Josephson junctions. The quantum soliton of the one dimensional attractive NLS model has been claimed to have been observed in a Bose condensed metal vapour ($^7Li$, ref.\cite{Ketal2k2}). Detailed calculations seem to suggest that the Bose condensate undergoes a 'collapse' from three to one space dimensions, thus yielding an exact soliton (``quantum soliton solution'') of the one dimensional quantum attractive NLS model.




\begin{thebibliography}{99}
\bibitem{RKB-MW2k4}
R K Bullough and Miki Wadati 2004. \emph{J. Opt. B: Quantum Semiclass. Opt.} 6: S205-S216. 

\bibitem{ABetal99}
Abraham (1999). Quantum Solitons. \emph{Phys. World}, February 21, 1999 issue.

\bibitem{Ketal2k2} 
Khaykovich, et al. 2002. Science. \textbf{296}: 1290.

\end{thebibliography}
